\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 0. Quick Revision Carried Over From Aug 22 (State vs. Context, Q&A follow-up)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 State is mutable
\f1\b0  \'97 tools/middleware can update it via returned dicts; it persists across turns 
\f0\b for the same thread ID
\f1\b0  (via a checkpointer).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Context is 
\f2\i not
\f0\i0  persistent across turns
\f1\b0  \'97 it must be supplied fresh on every 
\f3\fs26 .invoke()
\f1\fs24  call. It exists purely for 
\f0\b dependency injection
\f1\b0  into tools/middleware (avoids hardcoding values or using global state), which makes tools more testable, reusable, and flexible.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Correction from the previous session: you don't read 
\f3\fs26 runtime.state
\f1\fs24  directly from arbitrary code \'97 inside a 
\f0\b tool
\f1\b0  you access both state and context via the 
\f3\fs26 tool_runtime
\f1\fs24  parameter that LangChain passes in automatically.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 1. Why MCP Had to Exist \'97 The Origin Story\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 The instructor built this up as a deliberate narrative (his own teaching device) rather than jumping straight to a definition:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1.1 Stage 1 \'97 LLM as a pure "black box" brain\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 ChatGPT's Nov 30, 2022 launch is framed as the "dawn of the AI era" (comparable in scale to the launch of the internet) \'97 ~1M users in a week, ~100M within ~3 months.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Conceptually, an 
\f0\b LLM is just a next-token predictor
\f1\b0 : input text in, output text out \'97 a "black box brain" with no hands. Illustrated with a tokenizer example ("Hi, I am Mayank" \uc0\u8594  5 tokens).\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 At this stage it could only 
\f0\b describe
\f1\b0  actions (e.g., write a leave-application email) but never 
\f0\b perform
\f1\b0  them (e.g., actually send it). This is explicitly called out as the origin of the word 
\f0\b "Model"
\f1\b0  in MCP.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1.2 Stage 2 \'97 Context improves the answer, but the model still can't act\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Definition given:
\f1\b0  
\f4\i "Context is everything your LLM/model can see when it answers your question."
\f1\i0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Demonstrated live: asking Claude "who is the president of Venezuela \'97 don't search the internet" gives a 
\f0\b stale/wrong answer
\f1\b0  from training-data knowledge cutoff (said Nicol\'e1s Maduro, factually outdated per the demo); enabling web search and re-asking gives the correct, current answer.\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Key line (emphasized): 
\f0\b "MCP is not about giving our LLM more intelligence \'97 it's about giving it an ability to see more."
\f1\b0  This is the origin of the word 
\f0\b "Context"
\f1\b0  in MCP.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1.3 Stage 3 \'97 Function calling (tools) let the model 
\f2\i act
\f0\i0 , but doesn't scale\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Mid-2023: OpenAI shipped function calling \'97 you describe a set of functions (e.g., web search, read-email-via-Gmail, read-Slack-messages) and bind them to the LLM; the model decides which to call and with what arguments.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Crucial nuance: 
\f0\b the LLM itself never executes the function
\f1\b0  \'97 it only proposes a tool name + arguments; some other layer of code actually runs it. (Demoed live with Claude's calendar-check tool call, showing it emits structured args like start_time/end_time/timezone/order_by/page_size \'97 "very close to a real API call.")\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 These tool-wrapped actions are, in the backend, almost always just 
\f0\b thin wrappers around existing REST APIs
\f1\b0  (e.g., "read email" = Gmail's read API; demoed the Google Calendar API docs showing 
\f3\fs26 create event
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 list event
\f1\fs24  endpoints).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The new problem (why this "solution" was short-lived):
\f1\b0  at individual-developer scale, function calling is great. At 
\f0\b company scale
\f1\b0 , each team/app ends up writing, authenticating, and maintaining its own hand-rolled wrapper for every API, and this multiplies fast \'97 e.g., 4 applications \'d7 6 actions each = 24 separately-maintained tools. Concretely:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Massive 
\f0\b duplicate code
\f1\b0  across projects/companies (violates DRY \'97 "Don't Repeat Yourself").\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Redundant, non-scalable
\f1\b0  \'97 500 teams could all independently write near-identical Gmail-send code.\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Prone to breakage
\f1\b0  \'97 if Google changes its API (new required field, deprecated param, etc.), every hand-written wrapper everywhere has to be fixed.\
\ls4\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Security/auth overhead
\f1\b0  multiplies \'97 every tool needs its own auth handling.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Net effect: 
\f4\i "We found a shortcut, but now we need a whole new job just to keep the shortcut running."
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 1.4 Stage 4 \'97 MCP (the fix)\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Anthropic recognized this maintenance/duplication problem and created 
\f0\b MCP (Model Context Protocol)
\f1\b0  as a new, purpose-built protocol \'97 analogous in spirit to how HTTP or FTP are protocols with an agreed "shared language."\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Official framing (from Anthropic's docs, read aloud): 
\f4\i "MCP is an open-source standard for connecting AI applications to external systems\'85 enabling them to access key information and perform tasks."
\f1\i0 \
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The instructor explicitly 
\f0\b dislikes the common "USB-C port" analogy
\f1\b0  for MCP, finding it oversimplified/confusing. His own framing: 
\f0\b MCP is fundamentally a way to bundle a collection of tools/actions
\f1\b0  (which are themselves usually API wrappers) 
\f0\b behind a standard protocol
\f1\b0 , so that any AI application (the "host") can plug into any compliant server without writing/maintaining that wrapper code itself.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Instead of a company building its own Gmail integration, 
\f0\b Gmail (or a maintainer) exposes an MCP server
\f1\b0  with tools like 
\f3\fs26 send_email
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 read_email
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 create_draft
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 create_tag
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 delete_tag
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 archive_email
\f1\fs24 , etc. Any AI host just 
\f0\b connects
\f1\b0  to that server and gets all of that functionality "for free," with the maintenance burden living on the server side, not the consuming application.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP "primitives"
\f1\b0  (the three things an MCP server can expose) \'97 
\f0\b Tools, Resources, Prompts
\f1\b0  (Resources and Prompts to be covered later in depth).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Practical proof-point shown live: Claude's own 
\f0\b "connectors"
\f1\b0  feature ("Manage Connectors" \uc0\u8594  add a custom connector by giving a 
\f0\b remote MCP server URL
\f1\b0 ) 
\f4\i is
\f1\i0  MCP under the hood \'97 connecting Gmail this way immediately grants many tools without any custom integration work by the user.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Strategic implication stressed: 
\f0\b an application's MCP server quality now matters for its own adoption
\f1\b0  \'97 if a tool/app doesn't ship a good MCP server, users/AI agents may simply migrate to a competitor that does, because "the better the MCP server, the better it integrates into the AI ecosystem."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 2. Before vs. After MCP \'97 Side-by-Side Comparison\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 2.1 Before MCP (raw API/tool wrapping) \'97 using Gmail "send email" as the running example\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Positives:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Very easy to 
\f4\i define
\f1\i0  a single API/tool \'97 e.g., asking any LLM for a "send email via Gmail" Python script produces usable code almost instantly.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Easy to understand in isolation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 Negatives:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Duplicate code
\f1\b0  across every project/company that needs the same integration.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Redundant
\f1\b0  \'97 violates DRY.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fragile over time
\f1\b0  \'97 any upstream API change (new required field, deprecated behavior, etc.) breaks every hand-rolled wrapper.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Security burden multiplies
\f1\b0  \'97 every individual tool needs its own auth handling.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Fundamentally 
\f0\b not scalable
\f1\b0  \'97 e.g., "if there are 500 teams, 500 teams will independently write the same send-email code."\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2.2 After MCP\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The AI application (LLM/agent) connects directly to an 
\f0\b MCP server
\f1\b0  (e.g., Gmail's) instead of hand-building the integration.\
\ls8\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Live demo: adding a Gmail connector to Claude via 
\f0\b Manage Connectors \uc0\u8594  Connect \u8594  select account \u8594  authorize
\f1\b0 , after which many Gmail tools become available with zero custom integration code.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Positives:
\f1\b0  easy to define/access, no duplicate maintenance burden on the consumer side, the server owner (Gmail, Notion, GitHub, etc.) bears the responsibility for keeping tools correct/up to date, scalable across many consuming applications.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Nuance repeatedly clarified in Q&A: if you're maintaining your own MCP server, you still own its maintenance \'97 MCP mainly removes duplication 
\f0\b across consumers of the same server
\f1\b0 , not maintenance for the server owner itself.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

