\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP follows a 
\f0\b client\'96server architecture
\f1\b0 , with one added layer: 
\f0\b Host
\f1\b0 .\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl1
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Host
\f1\b0  \'97 the AI application itself (Claude Desktop, Claude web, VS Code, Cursor, any custom Agentic app built with LangChain/LangGraph/AutoGen, etc.).\
\ls9\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Client
\f1\b0  \'97 a component the host starts up 
\f0\b internally
\f1\b0  (not something you typically see or write yourself when using an existing host like Claude/VS Code) that speaks the MCP protocol to a specific server. A host can start 
\f0\b multiple clients
\f1\b0 , one per connected MCP server.\
\ls9\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Server
\f1\b0  \'97 exposes Tools/Resources/Prompts and does the real work (often by calling the underlying REST APIs).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Analogy offered (repeated several times as 
\f2\i the
\f0\i0  preferred mental model, replacing the USB-C analogy):
\f1\b0  
\f4\i mobile phone (host) \uc0\u8594  SIM card (client) \u8594  mobile network (server)
\f1\i0 . The phone doesn't talk to the cell network directly; the SIM card is the thing that actually speaks the network's protocol on the phone's behalf.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Host and client "speak the same language" \'97 this shared language 
\f0\b is
\f1\b0  the MCP protocol itself (analogous to how HTTP is the shared language between web browsers and web servers, FTP for file transfer, etc.).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Two transport mechanisms
\f1\b0  for MCP servers (deeper coverage promised in a future class):\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl1
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 STDIO (standard input/output)
\f1\b0  \'97 typically for 
\f0\b locally running
\f1\b0  servers (the client starts the server as a subprocess on your machine and talks to it via stdin/stdout).\
\ls9\ilvl1
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Streamable HTTP
\f1\b0  \'97 for 
\f0\b remotely hosted
\f1\b0  servers.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 A third, older mechanism \'97 
\f0\b HTTP + SSE (Server-Sent Events)
\f1\b0  \'97 has been 
\f0\b deprecated
\f1\b0  as of the MCP spec update released 
\f0\b March 2025
\f1\b0  (though actual server-by-server cutover timing varies).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 General interaction flow: host's request \uc0\u8594  client asks the server "what tools/resources/prompts do you have?" \u8594  server responds with tool schemas (name, description, input schema, output schema) \u8594  client relays available tools to the LLM/agent \u8594  agent decides which tool to call and with what arguments \u8594  client sends the actual call to the server \u8594  server executes (often by calling a real backend API) \u8594  result flows back up the chain.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP is generally 
\f0\b framework-agnostic and LLM-agnostic
\f1\b0  \'97 it can be used with any LLM/host (Claude, ChatGPT via appropriate hosts, custom agents, IDEs like VS Code/Cursor, etc.), not something tied to any single vendor's model.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \strokec2 4. Extensive Live Q&A \'97 Clarifications & Nuances\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f4\i\b0\fs24 \cf0 (Curated for durable insight; conversational back-and-forth condensed.)
\f1\i0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP vs. plain tools/APIs:
\f1\b0  In the old approach, 
\f4\i you
\f1\i0  define and maintain the tool wrapper yourself. With MCP, you 
\f4\i connect
\f1\i0  to an existing server and just describe what you want \'97 the heavy lifting (calling the underlying API, auth, error handling, keeping up with API changes) moves to the server maintainer. "Outcome is the same, approach is totally different."\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP as "just a REST client"?
\f1\b0  Rejected as too reductive \'97 "it is more than that," referring to the standardized protocol/negotiation layer (tool discovery, schemas, resources, prompts) on top of raw request/response.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP vs. RAG:
\f1\b0  Explicitly different \'97 RAG is "a totally different way of providing context" (retrieval-based), not covered in this session (to be taught separately later).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do we need our own MCP servers if a vendor already provides one?
\f1\b0  Yes, when: (a) it's 
\f4\i your own
\f1\i0  internal/company application (no one else will build that MCP server for you), (b) you need a narrower/custom subset of tools than a vendor's full server offers, or (c) you want to wrap/aggregate multiple existing MCP servers behind your own restricted server for internal governance reasons (an "MCP aggregator" pattern raised in Q&A \'97 confirmed as valid: you can build your own server that internally calls other MCP servers/APIs and exposes a curated subset).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Can an MCP server be "extended" via inheritance/derivation (OOP-style)?
\f1\b0  No \'97 "it's not code" in that sense. However, an MCP server 
\f4\i can
\f1\i0  internally contain 
\f0\b its own MCP client
\f1\b0  that connects onward to another MCP server (nested client-inside-server pattern) \'97 described as 
\f4\i possible but uncommon in practice
\f1\i0 .\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Name collisions between tools from different connected servers:
\f1\b0  Can happen in principle, but is mitigated by good tool 
\f0\b descriptions
\f1\b0  (the more descriptive, the less ambiguity) and/or explicit host-side hints (e.g., telling VS Code/Claude explicitly which MCP/tool to prefer).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 How is an MCP server deployed/hosted?
\f1\b0  Exactly like any other server \'97 can run as a plain process, inside a Docker container, on ECS, etc.; framework used in the demo is 
\f0\b FastMCP
\f1\b0 . For local/STDIO servers, the host starts the process directly (e.g., 
\f3\fs26 uv run python server.py
\f1\fs24 ), whereas HTTP servers can be deployed on any compute (VPS, Cloud Run, GKE, etc.) similar to a normal API server. Vertex AI is unrelated to 
\f4\i hosting
\f1\i0  an MCP server \'97 it's an AI-model platform, not a general compute host for this purpose.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Publishing an MCP server:
\f1\b0  The instructor plans to demo, in a future class, publishing a custom MCP server as a 
\f0\b PyPI package
\f1\b0  so others can install and use it directly \'97 framed as the target depth level ("if you say in an interview 'I built and published an MCP server,' that's the depth I want you to have").\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do all tools/resources from every connected MCP server get sent to the LLM on every turn?
\f1\b0  By default, yes \'97 demonstrated live via Claude's "context" panel showing token cost breakdown (system prompt, custom tools ~18K tokens, custom agents ~869K, skills ~2K, messages) even for a simple "hi," attributable to just two connected MCP servers. More connected servers/tools linearly increases this overhead. 
\f0\b Optimization is possible
\f1\b0  (echoing the earlier LLM Tool Selector Middleware topic) \'97 but the 
\f4\i initial
\f1\i0  tool discovery listing from a server is not selectively filtered by the protocol itself; filtering has to be done at the consuming application/middleware layer, not by asking the server for a subset up front (the server just returns everything it has when asked "list tools").\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Can we intercept and filter the tool list before it reaches the LLM?
\f1\b0  Yes \'97 this filtering happens at the 
\f0\b client/host or middleware layer
\f1\b0 , not at the MCP server, since the server always returns its full tool list; the consuming application decides what subset to actually forward to the model (ties back to the LLM Tool Selector Middleware concept from the earlier module).\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 LangChain's 
\f5\fs26 MultiServerMCPClient
\f0\fs24  and runtime access:
\f1\b0  LangChain documents that MCP servers run in a 
\f0\b separate process
\f1\b0  and normally 
\f0\b cannot access LangChain's own runtime
\f1\b0  (state/context/etc.). LangChain addresses this gap with 
\f0\b "interceptors"
\f1\b0  \'97 a mechanism to inject user/runtime context (e.g., via a 
\f3\fs26 MultiServerMCPClient
\f1\fs24  with 
\f3\fs26 tool_interceptors
\f1\fs24 ) into requests sent to MCP servers, so the MCP tool call can be runtime-aware even though it executes out-of-process.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 RBAC / per-tool authorization:
\f1\b0  Normally, authentication/authorization is done at the 
\f0\b whole-server level
\f1\b0  (you authenticate once against the entire MCP server, then use whichever tools it exposes). Restricting individual tools within an already-authenticated server (e.g., giving a developer read+write GitHub access but a PM only read access) was raised as a real need \'97 the instructor believes it's 
\f4\i technically achievable
\f1\i0  (from a coding standpoint) even if not a standard, universally-documented MCP feature, and said he'd need to verify current spec support.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Host vs. Client \'97 worked example to remove ambiguity:
\f1\b0  Demonstrated using 
\f0\b VS Code + a documentation MCP server
\f1\b0  ("docs by LangChain"): VS Code (the host) starts its own internal MCP client when you add a server config; that client requests 
\f3\fs26 list_tools
\f1\fs24  from the LangChain docs MCP server (tools observed: 
\f3\fs26 search_docs_by_langchain
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 query_docs_file_system_by_langchain
\f1\fs24 , 
\f3\fs26 submit_feedback
\f1\fs24 ) \'97 the server responds with tool name, description, and input/output schema for each. Also demonstrated an 
\f0\b MCP Inspector
\f1\b0  tool as a way to see raw client\uc0\u8596 server JSON traffic (e.g., a 
\f3\fs26 call_tool
\f1\fs24  "execute_tool" request/response), used to visually confirm which side is the "client" versus which is acting purely as a stateless server (VS Code's chat UI itself has no independent client/host identity outside of the app \'97 "code means nothing\'85 it's just a server").\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Adding a locally-running (STDIO) MCP server to VS Code
\f1\b0 , live: created an 
\f3\fs26 mcp.json
\f1\fs24  config entry with a 
\f3\fs26 stdio
\f1\fs24  server type, command 
\f3\fs26 uv run python server.py
\f1\fs24 , and a working directory set to the local project folder; VS Code Chat could then discover and call the local server's tools (a 
\f3\fs26 warm_up
\f1\fs24  tool was tested) once the config was added.\
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Restricting an internally-hosted MCP server to your team only
\f1\b0  (raised as a use case: wrapping 100+ internal Swagger/REST APIs into an MCP server exposed only to your org): confirmed as reasonable and common \'97 you build/host your own MCP server and control its exposure/network access just like any other internal API server; multiple people on a team can point their VS Code/Claude configs at the same server URL.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP is 
\f0\b generally stateless
\f1\b0  for most implementations (though this can vary by server design).\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Confirmed: 
\f0\b A2A (Agent-to-Agent)
\f1\b0  will also be covered later in the course (mentioned in passing, not detailed here).\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 MCP tooling exists inside other frameworks too (e.g., CrewAI has its own MCP server-hosting capability) \'97 reinforcing that MCP itself is a vendor/framework-agnostic standard, even though individual frameworks build convenience layers around it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
